Seasonal Pricing Logic – STR Revenue Framework

Overview
This document outlines a simple, effective pricing strategy for short‑term rentals in seasonal markets. It is based on occupancy patterns, demand cycles, and guest behavior trends.

---

1. High Season (June–September)
Characteristics:
- Highest demand
- Short booking window (3–10 days)
- Strong competition but high willingness to pay

Pricing Logic:
- Base price: +25% to +40%
- Minimum stay: 3–5 nights
- Last‑minute discounts: none or minimal
- Weekend premium: +10% to +15%

---

2. Shoulder Season (April–May, October)
Characteristics:
- Moderate demand
- Longer booking window
- Guests are price‑sensitive but quality‑focused

Pricing Logic:
- Base price: standard rate
- Minimum stay: 2–3 nights
- Last‑minute discount: 5%–10%
- Weekly discount: 10%

---

3. Low Season (November–March)
Characteristics:
- Low demand
- Longer stays more common
- Guests compare value heavily

Pricing Logic:
- Base price: –20% to –30%
- Minimum stay: 1–2 nights
- Weekly discount: 15%
- Monthly discount: 25%–35%

---

4. Event‑Based Adjustments
Increase prices during:
- Holidays
- Local festivals
- Conferences
- Cruise ship peaks

Adjustment: +10% to +25% depending on demand.

---

5. Review‑Based Adjustments
If reviews are consistently 5★:
- Increase base price by +5% every 10–15 stays

If reviews drop:
- Freeze price increases
- Improve listing quality and communication

---

Conclusion
This framework provides a stable, predictable pricing structure that balances occupancy and revenue throughout the year. It is simple to maintain and easy to adjust based on real‑time demand.
